    "knowledge_point": "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form",
    "problem": "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-3v-4,\\,y=2u-4v-5.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=2x+ay+b,\\,v=x-y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$",
    "worked_solution": "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v-4 \\\\ 2u-4v-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v \\\\ 2u-4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-4\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x+4 \\\\ y+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x+4)+(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (y+5) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+4)+(-1)\\cdot (y+5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{3}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-y-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+(-\\frac{3}{2})y+\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=x-y+(-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+(-1)=-2.\n$$",
