    "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. For q-124235, the inverse matrix has second row (2,3/2) and translation (-3,-5/2), giving 1. For q-124228, a=-7, b=-1, c=3, giving -5. For q-124312, u=2x-(3/2)y+1/2 and v=x-y-1, giving -2. For q-124317, u=7x-5y+4 and v=10x-7y+5, giving 10. The affine matrices in q-162215 and q-162197 have determinants 2 and 17 respectively, so they preserve the pentagon's five vertices and map the rectangle to a parallelogram. The boundaries in q-161550 and q-161599 are the equality parabolas. The triangle's interior in q-161731 requires strict inequalities 0<y<(2/3)x. In q-71888, the interior is the open upper half-disk; its stated lower radial bound is redundant because y>0 already implies x²+y²>0. For q-120990, integrating velocity gives 81-18=63 displacement. For q-81507, x(2)=e+(e-1)=2e-1. The closed sets in q-71793 are I and II; those in q-71796 are II and III, because they contain all their limit points. For q-71533, the scalar projection is 2cos(5π/6)=-√3. For q-101528, the dot product is 4 and the norms are 7 and 3, giving 4/7-4/3=-16/21. Confirmation rests on captured worked solutions; no submitted-answer grades are provided.",
